<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lombardy</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Lombardy is a region of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> centred on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a>, one of the wealthiest parts of the peninsula. It came under Austrian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> rule in the eighteenth century and formed part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-lombardy-venetia">Kingdom of Lombardy-Venetia</a> after 1815. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> lost Lombardy in 1859 during <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italian-unification">Italian unification</a>, one of the setbacks that preceded the creation of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austro-hungarian-empire">Austro-Hungarian Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lombardy.jpg" alt="Lombardy" width="320" height="403" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: TUBS · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lombardy_in_Italy.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Lombardy lies in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alps">Alps</a> and the tributaries of the river Po. It is bordered by the Swiss cantons of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ticino">Ticino</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/graubunden">Grisons</a> to the north, by Trentino-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-tyrol">Alto Adige</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/veneto">Veneto</a> to the east, Emilia-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romagna">Romagna</a> to the south and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piedmont">Piedmont</a> to the west. About 47 per cent of its territory is plain, 41 per cent mountains and 12 per cent hills. The Po forms its southern border for about 210 kilometres,. The name comes from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-lombards">Lombards</a>, a Germanic people whose name derives from Late Latin Longobardus, perhaps meaning long beard.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The area has been settled since at least the second millennium BCE, and the rock drawings of the Camuni in Valcamonica are well preserved. In the centuries after the fall of the Western Empire it suffered destruction from a series of invasions. After 540 Pavia became the permanent capital of the Ostrogothic Kingdom. The last and most effective invasion was that of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-lombards">Lombards</a>, who migrated from the Carpathian basin in 568 in fear of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avars">Avars</a>. Their long-lasting reign, with its capital at Pavia, gave the region its name, and Pavia remained the capital of the kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> until 1024.</p><p>From the late 11th century Lombardy broke into many small autonomous <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/city-state">city-states</a>, the medieval communes. In the 15th century the Duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a> was one of the wealthiest states of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a>, and Milan and Mantua were centres of Renaissance culture, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leonardo-da-vinci">Leonardo da Vinci</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andrea-mantegna">Andrea Mantegna</a>. Lombard merchants and bankers traded as far as northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, and the word Lombard came to mean a banker, as in Lombard Street in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-pavia">Battle of Pavia</a> the Duchy of Milan became a possession of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburgs</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, while the east, including Bergamo and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brescia">Brescia</a>, was controlled by the Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a>.</p><p>Austrian rule was restored in 1815 as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-lombardy-venetia">Kingdom of Lombardy-Venetia</a>. The region was an important centre of the Risorgimento, with the Five Days of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a> in March 1848 and the Ten Days of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brescia">Brescia</a> in 1849. Lombardy was annexed to the Kingdom of Piedmont-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardinia">Sardinia</a> after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-italian-war-of-independence">Second Italian War of Independence</a> in 1859, in which it was the main <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatre</a> of battle, with fighting at Magenta and Solferino.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Lombardy is the most populous region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, with about 10 million inhabitants, and its capital and largest city is <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a>. Eleven of Italy's 58 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Sites</a> are in Lombardy.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Lombardy” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The war of 1859 ended with defeat at Solferino and the loss of <b>Lombardy</b>, and the expensive army and an unstable currency left the state close to bankruptcy.</blockquote><a href="/empire/austrian-empire">Austrian Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>…but the defeats of the 1850s and 1860s forced a reconstruction of the imperial structure: the loss of <b>Lombardy</b> in the Second Italian War of Independence (1859), and the Austro-Prussian War of 1866, which excluded Austria from German affairs after the defeat at Königgrätz and cost it Venetia.</blockquote><a href="/empire/austro-hungarian-empire">Austro-Hungarian Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The Duchy of Milan grew out of the signoria, or lordship, that the Visconti family had built over Milan and the cities of <b>Lombardy</b> since the late thirteenth century.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-milan">Duchy of Milan · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>At the Treaty of Campo Formio in 1797 Francis II gave up the Austrian Netherlands and <b>Lombardy</b> in return for Venice, and after defeats at Marengo and Hohenlinden in 1800 the Treaty of Lunéville of 1801 confirmed French control of the left bank of the Rhine and set…</blockquote><a href="/empire/habsburg-monarchy">Habsburg Monarchy · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>In alliance with Napoleon III of France, Sardinia defeated Austria in 1859 at Magenta and Solferino and gained <b>Lombardy</b>; in 1860 plebiscites joined Tuscany, Parma, Modena and the Romagna, while Savoy and Nice were ceded to France as the price of French support.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-italy">Kingdom of Italy · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Cavour’s diplomacy, from the Crimean War to the agreement with Napoleon III at Plombières in 1858, drew France into war with Austria and allowed the kingdom to absorb <b>Lombardy</b>, the central Italian duchies and, after Giuseppe Garibaldi’s Expedition of the Thousand, the south.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-sardinia">Kingdom of Sardinia · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
